'' 'this' DENOTES THE OBJECT, not the pointer, and two places disagreed.
''
'' tiko-lang types 'this' as a POINTER because that is what the ABI passes -- fbc's own C
'' spells the receiver 'struct $1T*' -- and every member access reaches through it, so
'' the modelling is right everywhere except where the source's POINTER-NESS is what the
'' rule turns on.
''
'' TYPING: 'cast( Base, this )' was refused, because a pointer reaches a UDT
'' destination nowhere else. So the assignment target had no type at all and the
'' statement declined. Measured, and the distinction is real: 'cast( Base, *p )' over a
'' derived OBJECT is accepted while 'cast( Base, p )' over the POINTER is fbc error 20,
'' so only the receiver is unwrapped.
''
'' ADDRESS: with the typing fixed the statement lowered and was WRONG -- 'store @this,
'' <the loaded udt>' wrote the base object OVER THE RECEIVER SLOT instead of through
'' it. The object's address is the pointer's VALUE; its own address is a pointer to a
'' pointer. Caught by reading the IR against fbc's 'memcpy( THIS, U2, 8 )' before it
'' went anywhere.
''
'' structs/udt-init-2.bas writes every base assignment this way, inside a constructor.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/base-slice-assign.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' GOLDEN: expected/base-slice-assign.out
